  "generated_text": "To find the sum of the squares of the solutions to the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 + 4x - 1 = 0\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the coefficients**: The quadratic equation is in the form \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 4\\), and \\(c = -1\\).\n\n2. **Use the quadratic formula**: The solutions to the quadratic equation are given by:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n   \\]\n   Plugging in the values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{4^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (-1)}}{2 \\cdot 2}\n   \\]\n   Simplify inside the square root:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 8}}{4}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{24}}{4}\n   \\]\n   Simplify \\(\\sqrt{24}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{24} = 2\\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   So the solutions are:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-4 \\pm 2\\sqrt{6}}{4}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the fraction:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-4}{4} \\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{4}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = -1 \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the solutions are:\n   \\[\n   x_1 = -1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x_2 = -1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the sum of the solutions**: The sum of the solutions \\(x_1\\) and \\(x_2\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   x_1 + x_2 = \\left(-1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right) + \\left(-1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   x_1 + x_2 = -1 - 1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1 + x_2 = -2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Find the product of the solutions**: The product of the solutions \\(x_1\\) and \\(x_2\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   x_1 x_2 = \\left(-1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right) \\left(-1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Use the difference of squares formula:\n   \\[\n   x_1 x_2 = (-1)^2 - \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)^2\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   x_1 x_2 = 1 - \\frac{6}{4}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1 x_2 = 1 - \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1 x_2 = \\frac{2}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1 x_2 = -\\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Find the sum of the squares of the solutions**: The sum of the squares of the solutions can be found using the identity:\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + x_2^2 = (x_1 + x_2)^2 - 2x_1 x_2\n   \\]\n   Substitute the known values:\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + x_2^2 = (-2)^2 - 2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + x_2^2 = 4 + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + x_2^2 = 5\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the squares of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
